const v18ReadA={id:"v18-read-a",kind:"passage",label:"Passage for Items 69-72",title:"Medicine Delivery Loggers",description:"Vaccines and some other medicines lose their effectiveness if they become too warm or too cold, and a damaged vaccine may look exactly like a good one. The network's delivery routes cover mountain villages that can take most of a day to reach, often on rough roads in hot weather.\n\nA rural health network delivered temperature-sensitive medicine in insulated boxes.\n\nA box could leave the depot cold and arrive cold even if it had warmed for an hour in the middle of the trip. Health workers had no way to know whether this had happened, and they were unwilling to discard medicine without evidence.\n\nDrivers recorded departure and arrival temperatures, but the records could not show when a temperature excursion occurred during a six-hour trip. The network added electronic loggers that recorded every ten minutes and placed one probe beside the medicine rather than near the ice pack. Data showed repeated warming during long unloading stops. Routes were reorganized and boxes stayed closed until the receiving worker was ready. Excursions declined, although the network continued biological-quality checks because temperature history alone did not directly measure medicine potency.",alt:"Passage about medicine deliveries improved by ten-minute temperature loggers, correct probe placement, route changes, and closed boxes, while retaining separate potency checks."};
const v18ReadB={id:"v18-read-b",kind:"passage",label:"Babasahin para sa Aytem 73-76",title:"Mas Bukas na Pulong sa Badyet",description:"Taon-taong idinaraos ng munisipyo ang konsultasyon upang marinig ang mungkahi ng mga residente bago aprobahan ang badyet. Dito napag-uusapan kung aling kalsada, health center, o programa sa kabuhayan ang uunahin.\n\nKaunti ang dumadalo sa konsultasyon sa badyet na laging ginagawa tuwing hapon sa munisipyo.\n\nNapansin ng mga tagapag-organisa na halos iisang grupo ng tao ang dumadalo taon-taon, karamihan ay mga opisyal ng barangay at mga residenteng malapit sa munisipyo.\n\nSa panayam, sinabi ng ilang magulang na wala silang mapag-iiwanan ng bata, samantalang ang mga manggagawa sa malalayong barangay ay hindi nakaaabot bago magsara ang biyahe. Sinubukan ng bayan ang isang pulong sa Sabado, naglaan ng child-friendly waiting area, at nagbukas ng sabayang online at telephone comments. Dumami at naging mas iba-iba ang kalahok. Gayunman, hindi itinuring na awtomatikong kinatawan ng buong bayan ang mga dumalo; inihambing pa rin ang kanilang barangay at sektor sa kabuuang populasyon.",alt:"Teksto tungkol sa konsultasyon sa badyet na binago ang oras at access channels, nagdagdag ng child-friendly area, dumami ang kalahok, ngunit sinuri pa rin kung kinatawan sila ng buong bayan."};
const v18ReadC={id:"v18-read-c",kind:"passage",label:"Passage for Items 77-80",title:"Pedestrian Signal Timing",description:"The avenue is six lanes wide and runs between a public market and a hospital, so many of the people crossing it are older adults, patients, and shoppers carrying heavy bags. Signal timing had been set years earlier using a single assumed walking speed.\n\nA transport office received complaints that older adults could not finish crossing a wide avenue before the signal changed.\n\nEngineers found that some pedestrians waited a long time for the signal but then had too little time to cross, while others started crossing late because they could not tell how much time was left. Treating both as a single delay would have hidden this difference.\n\nEngineers measured walking time for a varied sample, including people using canes and wheelchairs, and separated waiting time from crossing time. They lengthened the pedestrian phase and added an audible countdown at two test intersections. In the pilot, incomplete crossings fell, while vehicle delay rose slightly. Officials planned to compare safety gains, accessibility, and traffic effects before expanding the change instead of treating vehicle speed as the only performance measure.",alt:"Passage about measuring diverse pedestrian crossing times, extending signals, adding audible countdowns, reducing incomplete crossings, and balancing safety and access with traffic delay."};
